 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186267414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268696 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a30a187b-9301-5273-a095-e7add20e9085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33686134-3f7d-5a05-9109-0d2ac79b776f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268929 #edb/ref 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9379a450-3ef7-54fa-b6cb-c35ea0f78eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268930 #edb/ref 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & ={e}^{yz}\\cdot (\\frac{1\\cdot (x+y)-x\\cdot 1}{(x+y)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y{e}^{yz}}{(x+y)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-\\frac{z{e}^{yz}}{x+y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\cdot ({e}^{yz}+yz{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{yz}(1+yz)}{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}}{(1+1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =-\\frac{{e}^{1\\cdot 0}(1+1\\cdot 0)}{1+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,1,0)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,1,0)} & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385357 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385359 #edb/ref 17592186045417]